3. What we expect

The key result should be:

Patient A  | NORMAL   | s1 -> s3 -> s4
Patient C  | CRITICAL | s1 -> s2 -> s4

NORMAL traffic cares strongly about utilization, so it avoids the 90%-utilized upper route.

CRITICAL traffic places much greater weight on delay, jitter, and loss, so it can select the faster/reliable upper route despite its utilization.

URGENT sits between those policies.

This is exactly what we need from Step 20:

$$ \boxed{ \text{Criticality} \rightarrow \text{QoS weights} \rightarrow \text{link cost} \rightarrow \text{route} } $$

But remember: these link metrics are controlled test values in Step 20. We are not going to falsely call this real-time routing yet.

